#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134864} :activity/id #uuid "80a63c78-ab52-5147-b1df-b7736290c5a2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050567 :topic/id #uuid "54747772-1cac-572e-9b8f-be3a6c8abe45" :topic/math-academy-id 2198} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134864 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135131 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf F =P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ and a region $D$ is bounded by a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve $C,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/fa/fa3efbc9200a6acdda01e503999f7588cd44fd63af77fa0a6abf1b5303c9a889.png)\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now consider what happens when the scalar curl term on the left-hand side of the above equation equals unity everywhere on $D.$ In other words, let's consider what happens when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the double integral gives the area of $D,$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=\\text{Area}(D).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThus, we've just discovered a new method of computing the area of $D.$ We simply choose a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ whose scalar curl equals unity everywhere on $D,$ and then compute its circulation over $C.$\n\nThere are infinitely many vector fields that we can use to compute the area of a region. Some of the most common for this purpose are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)= \\left\\langle -y, \\: 0 \\right\\rangle, \n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)= \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{y}{2}, \\: \\dfrac{x}{2} \\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y)= \\left\\langle 0, \\: x \\right\\rangle\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bbaebc78-d8b2-5500-9c52-f82bd3d3a8a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5839 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "25733548-0d69-5314-abb7-7cf1ba40a7bc" :step/math-academy-id 35784 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134868}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134865 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133895 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133711 :question/id #uuid "a817c3c6-97bb-55e4-9a1e-04ed99d3eaab" :question/math-academy-id "e-9553"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f4f726e-d985-5915-a4d0-30cfa6f4b89b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051070}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431050}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Satisfy the Unit Curl Property"} :step/id #uuid "31e81ee5-877c-5590-999c-e1a635893093" :step/math-academy-id 35785 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134866}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134866 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133896 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133712 :question/id #uuid "74c9ecfb-5005-53ff-b7a9-db798488587b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9554"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8644454b-ee25-59fd-ae65-9770a0b44442" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050666}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431053}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Bounded by a Closed Curve Using Green’s Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "3ff52bb4-279b-55a3-9202-9e4939a84e2e" :step/math-academy-id 35786 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134867}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134867 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133897 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133713 :question/id #uuid "aa4400ff-09b0-596c-9857-c13881b72f73" :question/math-academy-id "e-9555"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431052}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "0aca8ded-b498-5e7b-a4b3-06277e60cbf3" :step/math-academy-id 35787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134868 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135132 :tutorial/content "As a concrete example, let $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C,$ parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle 2\\cos t, \\: \\sin t \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in \\left [0, 2\\pi \\right).\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its positively oriented bounding curve $C$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6d9fe9cad4ccee56d5d61457c475b41371e0705f1aeccd3a18fb4a3fa8e127c.png)\n\nTo calculate the area of $D$ using Green's theorem, we need to find a vector field whose scalar curl equals unity everywhere on $D.$ So, let's choose\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = -y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = 0.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0 - \\left(-1\\right) = 1. \\qquad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $C$ is a positively oriented path, the area of $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting our expressions for $P$ and $Q$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y = -\\oint\\limits_C y \\: \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now evaluate the integral using the usual methods. The derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r'}(t) = \\langle -2\\sin t, \\: \\cos t \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo we can calculate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D)  =2\\pi.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f13baac9-fbd8-5a28-b168-f3010a604b1b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5840 :tutorial/title "A Concrete Example"} :step/id #uuid "f6584994-83e5-5672-aa98-e6e1491a031c" :step/math-academy-id 35792 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134876}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134876 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135137 :tutorial/content "We've established that if a region $D$ is bounded by a positively oriented curve $C,$ and we select a vector field $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ such that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} =  1,\n$$\n\nthen the area of $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) =  \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNow, if $C$ is *negatively* oriented, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) =  -\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = -\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5c61d2a3-2e54-5e8b-931c-beff07f3581e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5857 :tutorial/title "Negatively Oriented Curves"} :step/id #uuid "2f9c02ea-f6d5-5e40-8f6b-2625f2260d4c" :step/math-academy-id 35869 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134865}}] :activity/title "Using Green's Theorem to Calculate Area" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134040}]}
